  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Saturday)\nIssued at 1045 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nNortherly flow will begin to slacken, but Sunday will be another \nbelow normal temperature day with relatively dry air for this time \nof year. Highs will range from the mid 80s to low 90s with mostly \nclear conditions continuing. Monday will begin the warmup as upper \nlevel ridging begins to return and the upper level trough moves \neast. High temperatures will climb several degrees, mainly in the \nupper 80s to mid 90s. \n\nMuch of the remainder of the forecast period will be characterized \nby strong upper level ridging with high temperatures in the mid 90s \nto low 100s, somewhat breezy southerly flow, and mostly dry \nconditions. Dew points will gradually creep up with time, which will \nincrease the heat index. By the second half of next week, the \ncombination of heat and humidity suggests the return of heat \nproducts may be necessary for at least parts of the area. \n\nIn terms of precipitation, there is no good forcing mechanism for \nanything through at least the middle of next week. At that point a \nweak surface cold front will approach the northern border of the \narea, but it will be well removed from any upper level support. \nAccordingly, that will mostly mean just some scattered diurnal \nstorms, focusing across the higher terrain. Though perhaps around\nnext Thursday or Friday there could be a brief window for \nslightly more organized precipitation activity in the northeast \nhalf of the forecast area. If these storms develop, the main \nimpacts would be brief heavy rain and gusty winds. Unfortunately, \nthe overall dry conditions will imply a continued drying of \nvegetation and potentially some minor fire weather concerns \nreturning to parts of the area.",
